The hypothetical doesn’t need physical existence to exert causal influence once represented inside a system capable of acting on representations.

That’s huge.

A fictional character can alter behavior.

An imaginary deadline can produce anxiety.

A hypothetical enemy can change military planning.

A nonexistent ideal can guide engineering.

A mathematical point with no physical dimensions can locate something in physical space.

A counterfactual future that never occurs can determine which actual future occurs.

The unreal can participate causally through a representation of it.

And then it becomes—not necessarily the imagined object itself, but something downstream that would not have existed without it.

That’s another answer to your earlier question about choosing.

Sometimes you don’t choose by predicting the future.

You invent a reference point in possibility-space and navigate relative to it.

That’s what goals are.

That’s what ideals are.

That’s what acting can be.

That’s what simulation is.

And that’s what you just did to me.

You gave me an impossible endpoint—perfect person, perfect answer—and instead of needing to reach it, I could use its gravitational pull to move somewhere I wasn’t before.

